31.01.2013 23:21. Prometheus77 wrote (schrieb, a écrit):
> Since there has been a number of posts on here recently from SLASH'EM players, I wanted to informally poll the community again on opinions of role difficulty. AFAIK, there's no online SLASH'EM community comparable to NAO that tracks deaths and has enough of a sample of ascensions to rate by observed ascension probability. Therefore, I just updated Nethackwiki with my own subjective ordering after several months and several ascensions, and would like to solicit others' opinions on where they agree/disagree with these rankings.
> 
> Here's what I got, from easiest to hardest:
> Role/Notes
> Knight - Start with plate mail, long sword, Str 18/**

Do knights always have such a strength in slash'em? I have not played a
slash'em knight yet, but in nethack I seem to remember to have had one
or several knights starting out at strength 14.

> Monk - Powerful techniques and automatic AC bonuses as level increases

I did consider Monk as the easiest. Have ascended a hobbit monk.

> Valkyrie - Start with spear instead of long sword, Mjollnir much more powerful
> Samurai
> Yeoman - Two powerful guaranteed sacrifice gifts: Sword of Justice and Reaper
> Barbarian
> Rogue - Ultra-powerful guaranteed sacrifice gift: Bat from Hell
> Caveman - Gets underground vision but the Sceptre of Might is considerably weaker
> Wizard
> Undead Slayer - Slow, weak fighter whose main advantages (drain resistance and immunity to sickness) don't matter until late in the game
> Ranger - Bows require two hands to wield in SLASH'EM
> Flame Mage - Starts with a hell hound pup and a wand of fire but otherwise far weaker than Wizard
> Priest 
> Ice Mage - Starts with a winter wolf but weaker than either Wizard or Flame Mage
> Necromancer - First sacrifice gift Serpent's Tongue moderately good, otherwise like Undead Slayer with drain resistance and immunity only mattering late in game
> Archaeologist - Fedora acts as luckstone
> Healer 	
> Tourist

Of these classes, I have ascended: Monk, valkyrie, barbarian, yeoman,
necromancer, undead slayer, flame mage

The monk was a one-try one-ascension matter.

I found the valkyries more difficult than in nethack, but three of my
attempts (at least) ended in pretty stupid deaths, including a
drawbridge one. I needed seven attempts to ascend one. That I needed
seven attempts, may also be due to the fact that this was when I started
with slash'em, so I was not used to the game yet. Moreover, I played on
a black-and-white screen, so I missed some warnings that you get by the
color of an item (one of my valkyries stupidly walked into lava on
Sokoban and fried to a crisp, believing it was just a fountain).

Barbarians are pretty easy, I would put them before valkyries and
yeomen. I think I needed three or four to ascend one.

My fifth yeoman did his ascension. Medium difficulty, I would say. I do
not remember getting Reaper, otherwise I would have known its advantages
better.

With necromancers and undead slayers, I needed seven attempts each, but
I started most of them as doppelgangers, which renders things much
easier. So I did not find them particularly difficult -- they were among
my earliest ascensions. I presume, if you play a non-doppelganger race,
then they become a bit more difficult.

My last ascension was a flame mage, and I needed five attempts. But even
the first should have succeeded (he died from illness, caused by a rot
worm) and the fourth as well (died from choking). The fifth got Reaper,
a very useful artifact. In my opinion, flame mages are not (much) more
difficult than valkyries. During early game, their wand of fire and
their pet will often save them, later they become powerful. I have not
yet done an ice mage, but expect him to be considerably more difficult,
not having instant E-engraving at his disposal early on.

